## Building a Memory Game

### The Game Concept

1. Computer shows a sequence of numbers
2. Player must repeat the sequence in order
3. Each round adds one more number
4. Game ends on first mistake

### Generating Sequences

```xiom
fn generate_sequence(round: Int, seed: &mut Int) -> Vec[Int] {
  let mut seq: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  let mut i = 0;
  while i < round {
    seed = (seed * 1103515245 + 12345) % 2147483648;
    seq.push(1 + (seed % 9));
    i += 1;
  };
  seq
}
```

### Checking Player Input

```xiom
fn check_sequence(target: &Vec[Int], player_input: &Vec[Int]) -> Bool {
  if target.len() != player_input.len() {
    return false;
  };
  let mut i = 0;
  while i < target.len() {
    if target.get(i).unwrap() != player_input.get(i).unwrap() {
      return false;
    };
    i += 1;
  };
  true
}
```

### Game Loop

```xiom
fn play_memory_game() -> Int {
  let mut seed = 123;
  let mut round = 1;
  let mut score = 0;

  while round <= 20 {
    let target = generate_sequence(round, &mut seed);

    let mut player_input: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < target.len() {
      player_input.push(target.get(i).unwrap());
      i += 1;
    };

    if check_sequence(&target, &player_input) {
      score += round * 10;
      round += 1;
    } else {
      break;
    };
  };

  score
}
```

### Difficulty Progression

| Level | Sequence Length | Time to Memorize |
|-------|----------------|------------------|
| Easy | starting at 3 | Long display |
| Medium | starting at 4 | Brief display |
| Hard | starting at 5 | Flash display |

Higher levels start with longer sequences and give less time to memorize.

